<p>Although a potter and teacher by training, Charles “Happy” Grantier spent much of his professional life pursuing different types of art. As the son of pioneering homesteaders, he worked as a rural schoolteacher all over the state while he created several pieces of pottery, ceramics, watercolors, oil paintings, pencil sketches, chalk drawings, block prints, hooked rugs, and wax sculptures. Like threads in the handweavings he also produced, he wove these elements into a single, elegant, and profoundly simple life.</p>

